#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054637 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f1f99d3-ebcb-50c6-a162-bd58f7d5ec92" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements in a Power Set"}] :question/id #uuid "a138e1a7-52c5-5763-975e-cab082266099" :question/math-academy-id "e-13199" :question/problem "Find $\\mathcal{P}(A)$ if $A = \\{ 2, 4, \\{ 8 \\} \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The power set of $A,$ denoted $\\mathcal{P}(A),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $A,$ including the empty set $\\emptyset.$\n\nSo, let's list all possible subsets of $A$ using a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/b3/b3af256806e0aeb0396564320800f908c66d5830ab46e676dfc51ceab24154f6.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram, the subsets of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\emptyset = \\{\\},\\quad \\{2\\},\\quad \\{4\\},\\quad \\{ \\{8\\} \\}, \\quad \\{2, 4\\},\\quad \\{2, \\{8\\}\\},\\quad \\{4, \\{8\\}\\},\\quad\\{2, 4, \\{8\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(A) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{2\\}, \\{4\\}, \\{\\{8\\}\\}, \\{2, 4\\}, \\{2, \\{8\\}\\}, \\{4, \\{8\\}\\},\\{2, 4, \\{8\\}\\}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054447 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e22a7b0-753b-51b1-8946-5d17456aa778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cardinality of a Power Set"}] :question/id #uuid "a72a78b7-eaef-59a3-a23a-82d291e8253a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13200" :question/problem "If $Y = \\{ 1, \\{ 3, 5 \\}, 7, X \\},$ then what is $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(Y)\\right|?$" :question/worked-solution "For any set $Y,$ we have $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(Y)\\right| = 2^{\\left| Y \\right|}.$\n\nIn this case, $\\left| Y \\right| = 4,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\mathcal{P}(Y) \\right| = 2^{\\left| Y \\right|} = 2^{4} = 16.\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\{ 3,5 \\}$ is a single element in the set $Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054448 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054639 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ec1dc0-4e21-5001-959e-ed35039ca00e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Cardinality in More Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "b8db07f6-cdbf-5d00-8f3c-6f8b07277ac3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13201" :question/problem "Given that $Y =\\{\\mu\\}$ and $Z =\\{1,2\\},$ find $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{P}(Y\\times Z))\\right|.$" :question/worked-solution "For any set $S,$ we have $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|}.$ Also, $|S \\times T| = |S| \\cdot |T|.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\left| Y \\right| = 1, \\qquad\n\\left| Z \\right| = 2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(Y\\times Z)| & ={2}^{|Y\\times Z|} \\\\  & ={2}^{|Y|\\,\\cdot \\,|Z|} \\\\  & ={2}^{1\\cdot 2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(Y\\times Z))| & ={2}^{|P(Y\\times Z)|} \\\\  & ={2}^{4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054449 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4954fe5-f1fe-590a-9540-280a14606a49" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/id #uuid "ceb40e91-12d8-5636-8b98-fa41aaca33b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13214" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}(\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\})  = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\} \\}$\n\n- $\\mathcal{P} (\\{ \\emptyset, \\{0\\} \\} ) = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{0\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\{0, \\emptyset\\} \\}$\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}( \\mathcal{P}(\\{y\\}))  = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{y\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{y\\}\\} \\}$" :question/worked-solution "The power set of $T,$ denoted $\\mathcal{P}(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $\\emptyset.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\} \\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{ \\emptyset, \\{0\\} \\} .$ So, its power set must be $\\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\{0\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{0\\} \\} \\}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set $\\{y\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{\\emptyset, \\{y\\} \\}.$ Therefore, $\\mathcal{P}( \\mathcal{P}(\\{y\\})) = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{y\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{y\\}\\} \\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e22a7b0-753b-51b1-8946-5d17456aa778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cardinality of a Power Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f87ce3ea-2e44-566e-9046-42997d83dec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371487} {:answer/id #uuid "2e79ece2-46b0-5248-ada9-8809e4f1dc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371488} {:answer/id #uuid "d948b64d-82a1-5417-9ca3-74c3bb7db176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371489} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c87382-1036-5603-a5d9-8e82172c1e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371490} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f8d4c1-e0b3-5770-a66a-4dafee82cadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d948b64d-82a1-5417-9ca3-74c3bb7db176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371489} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e2617a4-d46c-5b9f-91af-14610bdb6ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32b58d47-77da-59a3-a1da-88971d941837" :question/math-academy-id "q-105137" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are sets. If $|X|=2$ and $|Y|=6,$ then $|P(X)|+|P(Y)|=$" :question/worked-solution "For any set $S$, $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ \n\nIn this case, $|X|=2$ and $|Y|=6,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(X)|+|P(Y)| & ={2}^{|X|}+{2}^{|Y|} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{2}^{6} \\\\  & =4+64 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054639 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ec1dc0-4e21-5001-959e-ed35039ca00e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Cardinality in More Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d82cc69-03ca-5471-acc1-9da617683063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051225} {:answer/id #uuid "9feea6eb-19f7-556e-a7d4-b2886b796fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051226} {:answer/id #uuid "caa29fd2-036b-5b30-96a3-1cbbd9faf312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051227} {:answer/id #uuid "e41ae800-452b-5b16-9c41-afd50e8099c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051228} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2bf2aa-e983-5923-9372-c7185ed7ee8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051229}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4b13b447-85d3-5825-82c9-3a957f5e3596" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/id